Fillets: a corner of three stripes where one rolls on an edge of the face
it consumes and another's contact on that face reaches the edge only at
its other end: the blends cut by each other, meeting at one point
(mitcad#1530).

The roof slab rounded on all its edges with 0.9 or 1: at each eave's tip
the tip's rounding rolls on the obtuse edge (1650), and the eave's end
edge and the slope's top edge on the end face are rounded too. The corner
of the three (PerformThreeCorner) cut the end edge's blend from its point
on the obtuse edge (1651) to where its contact on the end face meets the
line the slope's two roundings ride on (0016), both at that blend's other
end; the corner there, with the underside's rider, cut it along a curve
that crossed the first one (up to 0.06 past it), and the end edge's face
was left a sliver whose wire crossed itself.

No ball rolls between the three faces at the tip (it would touch the
consumed eave). The three blends are cut by each other, as the union of
the three roundings' removal sets is: each pair along its intersection
from where their contacts meet (the end edge's and the tip's at the point
on the obtuse edge) to the point where all three surfaces meet, which lies
on the intersection of the end edge's and the tip's blends. The end edge's
blend then keeps the piece between that corner's two curves, the rider's
intersection and the underside's cut.

- threeCornerByInter (PerformThreeCorner, after the three SearchFD): where
  one stripe has its side towards another on curve E (rolling on E) and the
  other's contact on the common face (1651) reaches E at its far end, the
  three stripes' pairs facing each other, one SurfData each at the corner:
  the intersection of those two from that point to where the other's
  second contact meets the rolling blend, the third surface's cut of it
  (the point P of all three), and the three intersections from where each
  pair's contacts meet to P. Each stripe's end is its two curves (on its
  surface, the stripe in the data structure at that end, as the big one's
  end of PerformTwoCornerbyInter); its points and its contacts' parameters
  are those of its pairs. A corner of this kind that fails is a failed
  corner ("ThreeCorner : the blends do not meet").

